WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.100
We’ve made it to the 21st century in zombie
history.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.620
In my opinion, while the last 20 years have
produced some of the most innovative and prolific

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:09.620
zombie tales—&nbsp;

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.740
It’s been a complicated couple of decades.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.100
In part because there’s already hundreds
of years of zombie lore to work with,&nbsp;

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.160
from the legacy of slavery displayed in the
Haitian zombi and early Hollywood films,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.250
to the flesh-eating Romero ghoul in popular
culture.&nbsp;

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.289
There are a few societal changes that made
things different in how these two legacies

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:32.020
are carried on:&nbsp;

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.800
terrorism on American soil,&nbsp;

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.120
several concurrent wars, the recognizable
impacts of global warming, genetic engineering,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.330
and sudden viral outbreak.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:46.500
With these events came a new set of anxieties,
which emerge in zombie narratives.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.910
Zombies never died—they can’t after all—but
they certainly have enjoyed a rebirth this

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:52.910
century.&nbsp;

00:00:52.910 --> 00:01:04.250
It’s challenging to simply define the modern
zombie because there are so many different

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:10.030
versions, and it seems like the formerly fundamental
element of zombiism is no longer essential

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.610
- they don’t have to be undead, strictly
speaking, and even if they are, in some narratives

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.850
they can be cured or at least controlled
The 21st-century zombie could be described

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:26.890
as any humanoid being, living or undead, that
craves human flesh and cannot fully control

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.990
its basic instinct to consume.&nbsp;

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.830
But in many ways it’s an exercise in contradictions.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.110
While generally mindless, we’ve also seen
zombies who problem-solve.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:42.710
While most are violent—in some cases, even
born of violence—

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.770
some are likeable.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:52.930
They can be slow-moving and decaying, incredibly
fast and swarming, or anything&nbsp; in between.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:58.210
When, and maybe more importantly, WHY did
the zombie get so much range?&nbsp;

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.670
To answer that we need to figure out how zombies
came to dominate the popular imagination.&nbsp;

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:07.200
Let’s start at the end of the last century,
when some real-world events put terrorism

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.470
and bioengineered disease at the forefront
of our minds—and video games addressed these

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:18.860
realities in their zombie characters.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:24.600
In 1996, the third-person shooter, zombie-apocalypse-survival
game Resident Evil was released bya Japanese

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.300
video game company to unprecedented critical
and financial success.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:35.250
The game has ill intentioned chemists and
geneticists working in secret developing bioweapons

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.830
that are released into the world and turn
humans into zombies.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:45.451
Likely inspired by earlier video games like
Sweet Home, Resident Evil gave a name to a

00:02:45.451 --> 00:02:50.210
developing genre: “survival horror”.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.770
The new Zombie genres’s popularity and the
release of these games came on the heels of

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:59.900
terrorists in Japan released discharging deadly
chemicals and toxins into unsuspecting cities.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.189
1996 was also the year the arcade shooter
game, The House of the Dead &nbsp;was released,

00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:11.810
which featured, wait for it, zombies spawned
by irresponsible and unethical scientists.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:16.650
Both Resident Evil and The House of The Dead
introduced a new trait: their zombies moved

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.180
fast.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.550
With these incredibly popular zombie-battling
video games, the undead were officially entrenched

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.180
in the public eye.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:31.860
In fact, even the great zombie filmmaker George
Romero gives credit in this way; in a 2013

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.020
interview , he said, "I do think the popularity
of the creature has come from video games,

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.520
not film.”

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.349
So in the late ‘90s, we saw this conflation
of bioengineering and zombification played

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:46.069
out in the popular video games of the day.

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:50.760
And Japan wasn’t the only country showing
increased fear of bioweapons;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.200
The 1996 pipe bomb attack at Centennial Olympic
Park in Atlanta, Georgia marked the first

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:02.519
time bomb fragments were immediately sent
to the CDC for chemical and biochemical testing.&nbsp;

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:07.570
No toxic agents were found, but the event
marked a point when bioweapons became a real

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:12.200
threat in American thinking, both politically
and publicly.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:18.039
Chemical and biological weapons of mass destruction
became more prolific at the end of the 1990s.

00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:23.349
Academic publications and news outlets reported
that recipes for bioagents like botulinum

00:04:23.349 --> 00:04:27.699
toxin could be found on the Internet and made
at home.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:30.779
Weaponizing a lethal sickness became a new
reality.

00:04:30.779 --> 00:04:36.490
But it was the rise of natural pandemics,
such as SARS in 2003 and the H1N1 influenza

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:41.960
pandemic in 2009 that revealed how quickly
and easily a disease could spread throughout

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.449
the world.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:47.870
The subsequent rise in global popularity of
the zombie movie, particularly in the first

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:53.789
decade of the 21st century, offered a recognizable
figure upon which to impose this anxiety — the

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:55.400
pandemic zombie.&nbsp;

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:00.259
The pandemic zombie is categorized by the
use of a pathogen that causes a disease that

00:05:00.259 --> 00:05:06.229
mutates the living human into someone we might
not recognize as human—it’s a sick body

00:05:06.229 --> 00:05:11.879
no longer in control of itself and which poses
a threat of spreading its contagion to others.&nbsp;

00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:16.900
The first pandemic zombie film to hit big
screens premiered in March of 2002—Resident

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.589
Evil, a spin off of the video game series
of the same name.

00:05:21.589 --> 00:05:26.710
In this zombie narrative, the deadly corporate-made
Tyrant virus, or T-Virus, designed under the

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:32.339
guise of “genetic research,” is released
into the population.

00:05:32.339 --> 00:05:37.330
Its victims reanimate from death with serious
brain-damage, causing them to attack swiftly

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.620
and violently infecting others through bodily
fluids.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:46.259
Despite the existence of an antidote, as the
franchise revealed in the following five films,

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:48.580
the pandemic could not be stopped.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.830
Resident Evil, the first film, grossed more
than $100 million dollars worldwide.&nbsp;

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:59.199
In 28 Days Later, which was released the same
year, the “RAGE virus,” in all its bloody

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:05.860
glory, solidified a whole new generation of
monsters, the fast moving, decay-free zombie.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:11.101
28 days later, zombies
or shattering for me, um, the idea of runners,

00:06:11.101 --> 00:06:14.569
uh, that was just something we had never seen
before earth shattering.

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:21.309
In fact, some people argue that you can’t
call the monsters in this film “true zombies,”

00:06:21.309 --> 00:06:25.449
because the diseased humans who hunger for
flesh and violence and suffer some loss of

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:31.740
brain function, are not technically reanimated
dead bodies, and therefore are not really

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:32.740
zombies.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:33.740
But I disagree.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.439
The mindlessness and lack of control seen
in these diseased characters, and often the

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:44.830
loss of personality and individuality, suggest
a loss of humanity, a defining characteristic

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:46.499
of the original zombis.

00:06:46.499 --> 00:06:52.419
Even if you take like the list zombie like
zombie, like 28 days later, where it doesn't

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:55.309
even raise from the dead, it's just a viral
infection, but people saw that movie and they

00:06:55.309 --> 00:06:56.309
went, no that's on me.

00:06:56.309 --> 00:07:01.669
Um, and I think the reason for that is if
it's a body that is no longer in control of

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:05.589
itself, that's just acting on autopilot and
drive.

00:07:05.589 --> 00:07:10.129
And very often that drive is to consume, um,
then that's zombie.

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:16.330
But the other part of it is that you have
the mob, which is such a trope.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.279
I mean, it's very, very rare to get alone
zombie film.

00:07:20.279 --> 00:07:24.789
Violence itself actually created the infected
hordes in 28 Days Later.

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:29.339
The Rage virus is a bloodborne disease that
resulted from scientists’ attempts to cure

00:07:29.339 --> 00:07:35.110
anger in humans by conducting primate research
with genetically engineered Ebola virus.&nbsp;

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:36.110
“The chimps are infected.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.889
Infected with what?

00:07:38.889 --> 00:07:40.139
Rage.”

00:07:40.139 --> 00:07:44.669
When an “eco-terrorist” group — releases
the laboratory’s chimpanzees, the chimps

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:51.009
go berserk, biting and infecting every human
in the lab, who in turn spread this Rage virus

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:52.749
around Great Britain.&nbsp;

00:07:52.749 --> 00:07:57.689
The movie’s eerie scenes of an abandoned
London, with newspapers bearing alarming headlines

00:07:57.689 --> 00:08:03.099
strewn throughout the street and countless
missing posters on the sides of buildings,

00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:08.430
uncannily call to mind similar images of the
real New York City in the days following the

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.509
collapse of the Twin Towers after the terrorist
attacks of 9/11.

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:18.900
It can be argued that these scenes, released
so soon after the devastating experience,

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.969
served as both a reminder of the tragedy,
and as a catharsis for the audience.

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:28.740
The zombies in this film might as well be
already dead, so killing them is a way for

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:34.120
audiences to project their own need for justice
onto non-human bodies.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:39.520
For the next decade, the majority of mainstream
zombie films followed the same basic premise

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:45.500
as these two films, demonizing scientific
intervention as the harbinger of the apocalypse,

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.600
usually aided by corporate greed or terrorist
activity.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.741
28 Weeks Later (2007), [REC] (2007), its American
remake Quarantine (2008),and Dead Girl (2008)

00:08:53.741 --> 00:08:59.930
to name only a few of many early zombie narratives
from the 21st-century, all contain some element

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:04.690
of scientific misjudgement as the cause of
the zombie plague.

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:11.070
In the first decade of the 21st century, scientific
intervention had become synonymous with death.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:15.050
The first “zombie walk,” where people
dress up as zombies and other related characters

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:20.140
to parade down city streets, occurred in Toronto
in 2003.&nbsp;

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:25.660
That same year, Max Brooks published his satire
on survival handbooks, The Zombie Survival

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:26.660
Guide.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:30.900
Thinking about what you would do to survive
the impending zombie apocalypse was nothing

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:36.430
new for horror fans, but the mass-produced
book flew off shelves, introducing different

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:41.250
audiences to the undead, alongside some really
practical survival tips.&nbsp;

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.630
There's a lot that we can learn from horror.

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:47.740
But it's definitely a
good idea to have a zombie bag somewhere. 

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:52.086
Because I think a lot of us have figured out we do to a degree, all of us have to be survivalist

00:09:52.086 --> 00:09:56.540
in the sense that we have to be ready in case.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.853
And when, and by the way, things are going
wrong.&nbsp;

00:09:59.853 --> 00:10:05.630
Shaun of the Dead (2004) featured Romero-style
zombies in a dark comedy—makes the slow-moving,

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:10.760
flesh-eating undead funny, offsetting blood
with humor.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.900
Zombieland (2009) and Warm Bodies (2013) would
later follow suit with laughs and some pretty

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:16.130
brutal gore.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:29.630
It kind of crazy that we’ve gone from zombis
that represent slavery, to zombies that are

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:33.430
funny…

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:38.460
And I can’t do a modern zombie episode without
talking about The Walking Dead—but I can’t

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:41.770
really say if they’re pandemic zombies or
not.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:45.700
First released in 2003, the ongoing zombie
comic series features a more “classic”

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:50.620
homage to the Romero zombie—it’s even
in black and white like the original Night

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.400
of the Living Dead.&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:58.340
The comic’s success led to The Walking Dead
TV show, which itself inspired two spin-offs.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:01.314
And apparently 3 forthcoming movies.

00:11:01.314 --> 00:11:05.359
Then there was the amazing video game in 2012.&nbsp;

00:11:05.359 --> 00:11:09.276
The Walking Dead even has its own freaking
Monopoly edition!

00:11:09.276 --> 00:11:12.450
That’s when you know something has become
a cultural icon.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:15.840
I could argue that in the last 10 years there’s
been a shift in how science is portrayed in

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:21.360
a lot of the recent narratives, regardless
of the classification of zombie —survivors

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.790
turn to science more as a means of salvation
rather than destruction.

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:27.790
Maggie (2015),

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:28.790
pause
What We Become (2015),&nbsp;

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.300
pause
and Pride and Prejudice and Zombies (2016)

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:33.350
suggest the zombie virus evolved naturally.&nbsp;

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:40.030
Even the film adaptation of World War Z attributes
the zombie plague to nature, with one character,

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.030
a virologist, stating,

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:43.410
“Mother Nature is a serial killer.”

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.980
Other recent zombie films attribute their
flesh-eating monsters to an intentionally

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:51.030
spread sexually transmitted infection&nbsp;
an evil cell signal&nbsp;

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:55.980
and “a minor leak” from a biotech district&nbsp;
What all of these films show is that we now

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:01.770
fear humanity, or nature’s response to it,
more than we fear science.

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:06.870
We no longer see science as a weapon, but
as our only hope for survival against an increasingly

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.680
multiplying threat.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:10.480
And I mean that quite literally.&nbsp;

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:16.230
Another trend in many of the most recent pandemic
zombie narratives is the sheer volume of zombies

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.050
plaguing humanity.&nbsp;

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.960
The utterly chaotic scenes in World War Z
legitimately give me anxiety.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:28.680
While the zombies in Brooks’s original book
were slow-moving, reminiscent of Romero zombies,

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:32.050
Hollywood’s version took them in a totally
different direction.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:35.990
They are VERY, VERY fast and strong.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:41.590
And it’s not a hundred or even a few thousand
zombies, but literally hundreds of thousands—even

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:43.690
millions—on screen.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:48.380
I call this type of zombie the Hive zombie,
after the insect species these animalistic,

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.030
swarming hordes mimic.

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:56.040
These zombies surge across cities in minutes
overwhelm transportation in seconds and mow

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:00.090
down any obstacle in their way by sheer number.&nbsp;

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:04.270
This zombie is terrifying because it cannot
be stopped with brute force or isolation.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:10.510
The sheer number of people who are infected
or could be infected overwhelms any existing

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:12.810
institutional structures.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:18.250
Then COVID-19 happened, and while it hasn’t
turned us into zombies (yet), the formerly

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:22.720
fictional idea of a highly contagious&nbsp;
pandemic for which we were woefully unprepared

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.700
became a very harsh reality.&nbsp;

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:28.870
Where do you, what do you think zombies are
going to look like next, especially with COVID-19

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:38.172
Well, I've been waiting forever for zombies
to learn how to shoot guns because with all

00:13:38.172 --> 00:13:41.610
of the mass shootings, I thought, okay, that's
gotta be the next thing.

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:45.459
And I mean, there are some films like Crazies that are zombic, like zombie-like, um, and

00:13:45.459 --> 00:13:50.431
there's Land of the Dead where zombies learn how to use tools.

00:13:50.431 --> 00:13:55.390
The pandemic is just test driving the ecological catastrophe to come. 

00:13:55.390 --> 00:14:01.850
So I'm really expecting the eco-zombie, as I call it, to be a very big phenomenon.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:07.700
I think now zombie storytelling is probably
going to lean more into what those subtexts

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:08.700
are.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:14.671
Uh, what zombies mean in terms of relationships,
what zombies mean in terms of community, what

00:14:14.671 --> 00:14:20.020
zombies mean in terms of these fears, these
societal theories of change we're talking

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:21.020
about.

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:24.170
I think those are the zombie projects in the
future that will, that will thrive.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:29.110
Unfortunately, many of us are witnessing,
or even experiencing first-hand, the effects

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:34.000
of a global viral pandemic, thereby importing
a sense of prescience and poignancy to what

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.510
was once a fun distraction.&nbsp;

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:43.110
Horror is our socio-cultural syllabus.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:50.840
It reveals our foibles, it, it comments on
our both social and political world.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:56.420
And it does it bravely and boldly because
in a lot of ways, they're not, we're not expecting

00:14:56.420 --> 00:15:05.601
horror to be Saving Private Ryan or, um, Sophie's Choice.They're using those, their

00:15:05.601 --> 00:15:08.910
narratives to be brave and to be bold.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:10.690
And in some ways that's really liberating.&nbsp;

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:16.253
In 2018 Jordan Peele became the first black winner for best original screenplay 

00:15:16.253 --> 00:15:23.316
for his horror film Get Out, proving what many fans already knew, that there is power in telling these stories. 

00:15:23.316 --> 00:15:25.316
I think it's possible to even read the movie as a zombie metaphor. 

00:15:25.316 --> 00:15:33.367
Creative people can create metaphors out of anything, but the zombie is uniquely maleable.

00:15:33.367 --> 00:15:41.340
It just lends itself to communicating a message, beyond the apparent surface message of the story. 

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:46.670
Change always comes from the fringes and horror is the quintessential for fringe genre.

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:50.460
The great thing about horror is, you&nbsp;
know, traditionally this was a place where

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.730
people would start in filmmaking because you
didn't need a lot of money.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:54.730
That's the kind of innovation that has always
driven horror.

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:59.530
They just had an idea and they had their fears and they found a way to bring them to the screen.

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:03.550
Zombies are unique in the world of monsters because THEY ARE US.&nbsp;

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:08.990
While science says that life ends with death, human mythology has always grappled with this,

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:10.839
across history and cultures.

00:16:10.839 --> 00:16:17.870
We are fascinated, even obsessed, with the idea that we could succumb to a worse fate:

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:19.370
zombification.&nbsp;

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:24.740
Anyone can become a zombie, so we see ourselves, our loved ones, our entire communities reflected

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:27.980
in the images of those walking corpses.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:32.340
Because death is the greatest unknown and our deepest fear, we inherently dread what

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:33.930
comes next.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:39.720
And it is perhaps an innate horror, this idea that after death we might not be able to rest--in

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:41.589
body or spirit.

00:16:41.589 --> 00:16:47.020
The undead aren’t scary because they are deceased--but because they are restless, ceaseless,

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:48.020
and insatiable.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:53.100
It’s like our own bodies, our own loved
ones, can return to harm us… meaning no

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:56.100
one is safe.&nbsp;

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:01.210
We've covered the basics of some of the most&nbsp;important historical moments in zombie history,

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:03.940
but there's so much more to talk about.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:09.980
That's why, on October 30, Monstrum will air its first one-hour broadcast special on PBS—

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:13.669
Exhumed: A History of Zombies.

00:17:13.669 --> 00:17:18.169
Make sure you tune in to your local PBS station
on October 30th at 10/9 Central.

00:17:18.169 --> 00:17:24.230
Join me and other experts as we deconstruct
the significant and intriguing cultural impact

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:25.586
of zombie history.

